WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.548
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.848 --> 00:00:09.106
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:18.293
Unit thirty-seven: Ecology of the Mekong. In this unit, you will structure an argument about the future of the Mekong.

4
00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:20.633
Mekong basin.

5
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:26.293
Mekong basin means all the land drained by the Mekong River and its tributaries.

6
00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:30.055
More than sixty million people live in the Mekong basin.

7
00:00:30.755 --> 00:00:33.872
Your turn. Say: Mekong basin.

8
00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:37.701
Mekong basin.

9
00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:39.787
flood pulse.

10
00:00:40.487 --> 00:00:46.771
flood pulse means the yearly rise and fall of river water that drives the whole ecosystem.

11
00:00:47.071 --> 00:00:52.136
The flood pulse makes the Tonle Sap swell to several times its dry-season size.

12
00:00:52.836 --> 00:00:55.859
Your turn. Say: flood pulse.

13
00:00:58.559 --> 00:00:59.920
flood pulse.

14
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:02.162
fish migration.

15
00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:08.588
fish migration means the seasonal movement of fish between feeding and spawning areas.

16
00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:11.816
Dams can block fish migration along the river.

17
00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:15.621
Your turn. Say: fish migration.

18
00:01:18.321 --> 00:01:19.531
fish migration.

19
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:22.063
sediment transport.

20
00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:27.782
sediment transport means the movement of sand, silt and nutrients by a river.

21
00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:30.708
Sediment transport keeps the delta fertile.

22
00:01:31.408 --> 00:01:34.873
Your turn. Say: sediment transport.

23
00:01:37.573 --> 00:01:39.050
sediment transport.

24
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.481
delta subsidence.

25
00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:46.177
delta subsidence means the gradual sinking of a river delta.

26
00:01:46.477 --> 00:01:49.672
Groundwater pumping accelerates delta subsidence.

27
00:01:50.372 --> 00:01:54.197
Your turn. Say: delta subsidence.

28
00:01:56.897 --> 00:01:58.224
delta subsidence.

29
00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:00.787
saltwater intrusion.

30
00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:06.133
saltwater intrusion means sea water moving inland into rivers and farmland.

31
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:10.650
Saltwater intrusion has ruined rice harvests in the delta.

32
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.618
Your turn. Say: saltwater intrusion.

33
00:02:17.318 --> 00:02:18.934
saltwater intrusion.

34
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.508
floodplain forest.

35
00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:26.948
floodplain forest means forest that is flooded for part of every year.

36
00:02:27.248 --> 00:02:30.037
Fish shelter and feed in the floodplain forest.

37
00:02:30.737 --> 00:02:34.155
Your turn. Say: floodplain forest.

38
00:02:36.855 --> 00:02:38.309
floodplain forest.

39
00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:40.840
Irrawaddy dolphin.

40
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.955
Irrawaddy dolphin means a rare river dolphin found in the Mekong near Kratie.

41
00:02:46.255 --> 00:02:49.880
Only around a hundred Irrawaddy dolphins remain in the Mekong.

42
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.731
Your turn. Say: Irrawaddy dolphin.

43
00:02:56.431 --> 00:02:57.815
Irrawaddy dolphin.

44
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:01.002
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:01.302 --> 00:03:05.449
Rithy, if you wrote an essay on the Mekong, how would you begin?

46
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:12.105
First, I would establish why the river matters: food, farming and culture for millions.

47
00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:15.036
Good. And then?

48
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:21.182
Next, I'd explain the flood pulse, which reverses the Tonle Sap every year.

49
00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:23.577
And the threats?

50
00:03:24.883 --> 00:03:29.066
Turning to the threats, dams trap sediment and block fish migration.

51
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.641
How would you conclude?

52
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.527
In conclusion, I'd argue that the river must be managed as one shared system.

53
00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:43.487
Grammar focus: Discourse organisation in essays (extended).

54
00:03:43.787 --> 00:03:46.785
Signposting guides the reader through an essay.

55
00:03:47.085 --> 00:03:52.825
First, I would establish why the river matters. Turning to the threats, dams trap sediment.

56
00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:57.470
In conclusion, the river must be managed as one shared system.

57
00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:04.367
Signpost every stage of an essay: thesis, development, counter-argument and conclusion.

58
00:04:05.383 --> 00:04:08.686
Now read and listen. A River That Breathes.

59
00:04:08.986 --> 00:04:12.773
The Mekong is one of the most productive river systems on Earth.

60
00:04:14.083 --> 00:04:22.944
Its ecology depends on the flood pulse: each monsoon, rising water reverses the flow of the Tonle Sap River and floods the great lake.

61
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:28.955
Floodplain forests then become nurseries where fish feed and breed.

62
00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:37.358
Fish migration supports one of the largest inland fisheries in the world, a vital source of protein for Cambodians.

63
00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:44.376
Sediment transport carries nutrients downstream, nourishing farmland and building the delta.

64
00:04:45.683 --> 00:04:53.035
Turning to the threats, upstream dams trap sediment, while groundwater pumping contributes to delta subsidence.

65
00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:59.914
As the delta sinks and sea levels rise, saltwater intrusion damages rice fields.

66
00:05:01.217 --> 00:05:08.290
Even the Irrawaddy dolphin near Kratie is a reminder that the Mekong basin must be managed as a single living system.

67
00:05:09.583 --> 00:05:12.375
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:16.195
The yearly rise and fall of river water is the blank.

69
00:05:16.495 --> 00:05:22.192
A, flood pulse. B, delta subsidence. C, sediment transport.

70
00:05:26.183 --> 00:05:28.740
The answer is A: flood pulse.

71
00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:33.109
Which phrase signals a new section of an essay?

72
00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:37.614
A, Turning to .... B, For example .... C, As a result ....

73
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:44.173
The answer is A: Turning to ....

74
00:05:45.683 --> 00:05:49.076
Which phrase best introduces a final synthesis?

75
00:05:49.376 --> 00:05:53.633
A, In conclusion. B, Firstly. C, By contrast.

76
00:05:57.617 --> 00:06:00.313
The answer is A: In conclusion.

77
00:06:01.817 --> 00:06:05.105
Sea water moving inland into farmland is blank.

78
00:06:05.405 --> 00:06:11.195
A, saltwater intrusion. B, fish migration. C, flood pulse.

79
00:06:15.183 --> 00:06:18.425
The answer is A: saltwater intrusion.

80
00:06:19.917 --> 00:06:23.890
According to the text, where is the Irrawaddy dolphin found?

81
00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:30.014
A, near Kratie. B, in the Tonle Sap lake. C, in the South China Sea.

82
00:06:34.017 --> 00:06:36.248
The answer is A: near Kratie.

83
00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:43.909
Now it's your turn. Part one: How should Mekong countries balance hydropower and the health of the river?

84
00:06:49.209 --> 00:06:56.375
Part two: Organise your answer with clear signposts: introduction, two points, conclusion.

85
00:07:02.675 --> 00:07:16.359
First, hydropower brings electricity and income. Turning to the costs, dams block fish migration and trap sediment. In conclusion, countries should share data and plan dams together, protecting the river for everyone.

86
00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:19.169
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:20.183 --> 00:07:26.922
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse organisation in essays (extended).

88
00:07:27.222 --> 00:07:32.290
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.846
See you in Unit thirty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:36.146 --> 00:07:38.612
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

